"Effective context engineering for AI agents \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†News】Research Policy Commitments\nL3: \nL4: Company\nL5: \nL6: Try Claude\nL7: \nL8: [Input: Enter search...][Input]\nL9: \nL10: ### Chapter 01: Context engineering vs. prompt engineering\nL11: # Effective context engineering for AI agents\nL12: \nL13:   * 【2†01Context engineering vs. prompt engineering】\nL14:   * 【3†02Why context engineering is important to building capable agents】\nL15:   * 【4†03The anatomy of effective context】\nL16:   * 【5†04Context retrieval and agentic search】\nL17:   * 【6†05Conclusion】\nL18: \nL19:   * Published\nL20: \nL21: Sep 29, 2025\nL22: \nL23:   * Team\nL24: \nL25: 【7†Engineering】\nL26: \nL27:   * Topics\nL28: \nL29:     * 【8†Context engineering】\nL30:     * 【9†Agents】\nL31:     * 【10†Tool use】\nL32: [Button: See more...]\nL33: \nL34: Related articles\nL35: \nL36:   * Engineering\nL37: \nL38: Nov 4, 2025\nL39: 【11†Code execution with MCP: Building more efficient agents】\nL40: \nL41: 【8†View more】\nL42: \nL43: After a few years of prompt engineering being the focus of attention in applied AI, a new term has come to prominence: context engineering. Building with language models is becoming less about finding the right words and phrases for your prompts, and more about answering the broader question of “what configuration of context is most likely to generate our model’s desired behavior?\"\nL44: Context refers to the set of tokens included when sampling from a large-language model (LLM). The engineering problem at hand is optimizing the utility of those tokens against the inherent constraints of LLMs in order to consistently achieve a desired outcome. Effectively wrangling LLMs often requires thinking in context — in other words: considering the holistic state available to the LLM at any given time and what potential behaviors that state might yield.\nL45: In this post, we’ll explore the emerging art of context engineering and offer a refined mental model for building steerable, effective agents.\nL46: ## Context engineering vs. prompt engineering\nL47: At Anthropic, we view context engineering as the natural progression of prompt engineering. Prompt engineering refers to methods for writing and organizing LLM instructions for optimal outcomes (see 【12†our docs†docs.anthropic.com】 for an overview and useful prompt engineering strategies).\nL48: Context engineering refers to the set of strategies for curating and maintaining the optimal set of tokens (information) during LLM inference, including all the other information that may land there outside of the prompts.\nL49: In the early days of engineering with LLMs, prompting was the biggest component of AI engineering work, as the majority of use cases outside of everyday chat interactions required prompts optimized for one-shot classification or text generation tasks. As the term implies, the primary focus of prompt engineering is how to write effective prompts, particularly system prompts.\nL50: However, as we move towards engineering more capable agents that operate over multiple turns of inference and longer time horizons, we need strategies for managing the entire context state (system instructions, tools, 【13†Model Context Protocol†modelcontextprotocol.io】 (MCP), external data, message history, etc).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDemystifying evals for AI agents \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/research/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents)\nciteturn0view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/research/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 481\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†News】Research Policy Commitments\nL3: \nL4: Company\nL5: \nL6: Try Claude\nL7: \nL8: [Input: Enter search...][Input]\nL9: \nL10: ### Chapter 01: Introduction\nL11: # Demystifying evals for AI agents\nL12: \nL13:   * 【2†01Introduction】\nL14:   * 【3†02The structure of an evaluation】\nL15:   * 【4†03Why build evaluations?】\nL16:   * 【5†04How to evaluate AI agents】\nL17:   * 【6†05Going from zero to one: a roadmap to great evals for agents】\nL18:   * 【7†06How evals fit with other methods for a holistic understanding of agents】\nL19:   * 【8†07Conclusion】\nL20:   * 【9†08Appendix: Eval frameworks】\nL21: \nL22:   * Published\nL23: \nL24: Jan 9, 2026\nL25: \nL26:   * Team\nL27: \nL28: 【10†Engineering】\nL29: \nL30:   * Topics\nL31:     * 【11†Agents】\nL32:     * 【12†Measurement and mitigation】\nL33:     * 【13†Benchmarks】\nL34:     * 【14†Claude Code】\nL35:     * 【15†Tool use】\nL36: [Button: See more...]\nL37: \nL38: Related articles\nL39: \nL40:   * Frontier Red Team\nL41: \nL42: Apr 9, 2026\nL43: \nL44: 【16†Trustworthy agents in practice】\nL45: \nL46: 【11†View more】\nL47: ## Introduction\nL48: \nL49: Good evaluations help teams ship AI agents more confidently. Without them, it’s easy to get stuck in reactive loops—catching issues only in production, where fixing one failure creates others. Evals make problems and behavioral changes visible before they affect users, and their value compounds over the lifecycle of an agent.\nL50: As we described in 【17†Building effective agents】, agents operate over many turns: calling tools, modifying state, and adapting based on intermediate results. These same capabilities that make AI agents useful—autonomy, intelligence, and flexibility—also make them harder to evaluate.\nL51: Through our internal work and with customers at the frontier of agent development, we’ve learned how to design more rigorous and useful evals for agents. Here's what's worked across a range of agent architectures and use cases in real-world deployment.\nL52: ## The structure of an evaluation\nL53: \nL54: An evaluation (“eval”) is a test for an AI system: give an AI an input, then apply grading logic to its output to measure success. In this post, we focus on automated evals that can be run during development without real users.\nL55: Single-turn evaluations are straightforward: a prompt, a response, and grading logic. For earlier LLMs, single-turn, non-agentic evals were the main evaluation method. As AI capabilities have advanced, multi-turn evaluations have become increasingly common.\nL56: \nL57: 【18†Image】\nL58: In a simple eval, an agent processes a prompt, and a grader checks if the output matches expectations. For a more complex multi-turn eval, a coding agent receives tools, a task (building an MCP server in this case), and an environment, executes an \"agent loop\" (tool calls and reasoning), and updates the environment with the implementation. Grading then uses unit tests to verify the working MCP server.\nL59: Agent evaluations are even more complex. Agents use tools across many turns, modifying state in the environment and adapting as they go—which means mistakes can propagate and compound. Frontier models can also find creative solutions that surpass the limits of static evals. For instance, Opus 4.5 solved a 【19†𝜏2-bench†github.com】 problem about booking a flight by 【20†discovering】 a loophole in the policy. It “failed” the evaluation as written, but actually came up with a better solution for the user.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow we contain Claude across products \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/research/how-we-contain-claude)\nciteturn0view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/research/how-we-contain-claude\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 233\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†News】Research Policy Commitments\nL3: \nL4: Company\nL5: \nL6: Try Claude\nL7: \nL8: [Input: Enter search...][Input]\nL9: \nL10: ### Chapter 01: Three types of risk, three components of defense\nL11: # How we contain Claude across products\nL12: \nL13:   * 【2†01Three types of risk, three components of defense】\nL14:   * 【3†02Patterns for containing agents】\nL15:   * 【4†03Trusting what the agent reads】\nL16:   * 【5†04Looking ahead】\nL17:   * 【6†05Summary】\nL18: \nL19: 【7†Image: How we contain Claude across products】\nL20: \nL21:   * Published\nL22: \nL23: May 25, 2026\nL24: \nL25:   * Team\nL26: \nL27: 【8†Engineering】\nL28: \nL29:   * Topics\nL30: \nL31:     * 【9†Safeguards】\nL32:     * 【10†Agents】\nL33:     * 【11†Claude Code】\nL34:     * 【12†Cybersecurity】\nL35:     * 【13†Jailbreaks】\nL36:     * 【14†Reward hacking】\nL37: [Button: See more...]\nL38: Related articles\nL39: \nL40:   * Frontier Red Team\nL41: \nL42: Apr 9, 2026\nL43: \nL44: 【15†Trustworthy agents in practice】\nL45: \nL46: 【9†View more】\nL47: Twelve months ago, we'd have rejected out of hand the idea of granting Claude access sufficient to take down an internal Anthropic service. Today that level of access is routine, and Anthropic developers are more productive for it. The risk of these deployments has two components: how likely a failure is, and how much damage one could do. Progress on safeguards and model training has steadily driven down the first; the second—the theoretical blast radius—only grows as capabilities and access expand.\nL48: Yet as agents become capable of doing work that once required a person or even a team, the cost of not deploying grows large enough that the risk-reward calculation tips heavily toward adoption, as long as products can be made safe. The engineering question becomes how to cap the blast radius.\nL49: 【16†Image】\nL50: When bounds can be placed on the relative damage of an autonomous agent—such as through control over its environment—high-utility capabilities can motivate deployment. Claude Mythos Preview is an example of a model whose blast radius was deemed too high to ship in April 2026. However, we expect broader release of models with similar levels of capability to become appropriate as defenders harden critical systems and safeguards mature—even though some risk will always remain.\nL51: Model capability is an important factor in the total risk of an agent’s deployment.\nL52: There are broadly two ways to do this.\nL53: The first is to supervise the agent’s behavior via a human-in-the-loop. Claude Code previously protected against agents taking unintended actions by asking users for permission at each turn. Theoretically that works, but we’ve found the approach to be fallible. Our telemetry showed users approved roughly 93% of permission prompts. The more approvals a user sees, the less attention they pay to each, becoming over time much less diligent in their supervision.\nL54: We recently built Claude Code auto mode, which 【17†automates safer approvals】 in order to reduce this approval fatigue. Still, vulnerabilities remain—any probabilistic defense has a non-zero miss rate.^{【18†1】}\nL55: The second approach to capping the blast radius—and the focus of much of this post—is containment. Rather than supervising what the agent does, we supervise what it’s able to do by enforcing access boundaries through, for example, sandboxes, virtual machines, and egress controls. This is where Anthropic engineering has devoted the most effort, and also where many of the most surprising security failures have occurred.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe are Changing our Developer Productivity Experiment Design - METR (https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/)\nciteturn0view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0:   * Our Work\nL1: \nL2:     * 【0†Research】\nL3:     * 【1†Notes】\nL4:     * 【2†Updates】\nL5:     * 【3†Risk Assessment】\nL6: \nL7:   * 【4†About】\nL8:   * 【5†Donate】\nL9:   * 【6†Careers】\nL10:   * 【7†Search】\nL11: \nL12: 【8†Image: METR Logo】\nL13: \nL14:   * 【9†Our Work】\nL15: \nL16: 【0†Research】 【1†Notes】 【2†Updates】 【3†Risk Assessment】\nL17: \nL18:   * 【4†About】\nL19:   * 【5†Donate】\nL20:   * 【6†Careers】\nL21:   * [Input] [Input: Search...]\nL22: \nL23: [Button: Menu]\nL24: \nL25: We are Changing our Developer Productivity Experiment Design\nL26: ##### CONTRIBUTORS\nL27: \nL28: 【10†Joel Becker】, 【11†Nate Rush】, 【12†Tom Cunningham】, 【13†David Rein】, and 【14†Khalid Mahamud】\nL29: ##### DATE\nL30: \nL31: February 24, 2026\nL32: \nL33: [Input: Join our newsletter] [Button: Subscribe]\nL34: \nL35: METR previously published a 【15†paper†arxiv.org】 which found the use of AI tools caused a 20% slowdown in completing tasks among experienced open-source developers, using data from February to June 2025.\nL36: \nL37: To understand how AI is impacting developer productivity over time, we started a new experiment in August 2025 with a larger pool of developers using the latest AI tools.\nL38: Unfortunately, given participant feedback and surveys, we believe that the data from our new experiment gives us an unreliable signal of the current productivity effect of AI tools. The primary reason is that we have observed a significant increase in developers choosing not to participate in the study because they do not wish to work without AI, which likely biases downwards our estimate of AI-assisted speedup.\nL39: We additionally believe there have been selection effects due to a lower pay rate (we reduced the pay from $150/hr to $50/hr), and that our measurements of time-spent on each task are unreliable for the fraction of developers who use multiple AI agents concurrently.\nL40: Based on conversations with study participants, we believe it is likely that developers are more sped up from AI tools now — in early 2026 — compared to our estimates from early 2025. However, because of the selection effects in our experiment, our data is only very weak evidence for the size of this increase.\nL41: Our raw results show some evidence for speedup. Our early 2025 study found the use of AI causes tasks to take 19% longer, with a confidence interval between +2% and +39%. For the subset of the original developers who participated in the later study, we now estimate a speedup of -18% with a confidence interval between -38% and +9%. Among newly-recruited developers the estimated speedup is -4%, with a confidence interval between -15% and +9%.\nL42: 【16†Image: Late-2025 AI likely accelerated open-source developers, but selection effects obscure the true speedup.】\nL43: \nL44: However the true speedup could be much higher among the developers and tasks which are selected out of the experiment. Some developers self-report very high speedups, though as we documented in our earlier study those estimates can be quite unreliable.\nL45: Due to the severity of these selection effects, we are working on changes to the design of our study. Below, we provide further detail and describe our plans for other means of studying the impact of AI on developer productivity.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n8.1.3.1. Censoring (https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/apr/section1/apr131.htm)\nciteturn0view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/apr/section1/apr131.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 20\nL0: 【0†Image】\nL1: 8. 【1†Assessing Product Reliability】\nL2: 8.1. 【2†Introduction】\nL3: 8.1.3. 【3†What are some common difficulties with reliability data and how are they overcome?】\nL4: \nL5: ## 8.1.3.1.\nL6: ## Censoring\nL7: When not all units on test fail we have censored data  | Consider a situation in which we are reliability testing \\(n\\) (non-repairable) units taken randomly from a population. We are investigating the population to determine if its failure rate is acceptable. In the typical test scenario, we have a fixed time \\(T\\) to run the units to see if they survive or fail. The data obtained are called Censored Type I data.\nL8: Censored Type I Data During the \\(T\\) hours of test we observe \\(r\\) failures (where \\(r\\) can be any number from 0 to \\(n\\)). The (exact) failure times are \\(t_1, \\, t_2, \\, \\ldots, \\, t_r\\), and there are \\((n - r)\\) units that survived the entire \\(T\\)-hour test without failing. Note that \\(T\\) is fixed in advance and \\(r\\) is random, since we don't know how many failures will occur until the test is run.\nL9: Note also that we assume the exact times of failure are recorded when there are failures.  This type of censoring is also called \"right censored\" data since the times of failure to the right (i.e., larger than \\(T\\)) are missing. Another (much less common) way to test is to decide in advance that you want to see exactly \\(r\\) failure times and then test until they occur. For example, you might put 100 units on test and decide you want to see at least half of them fail.\nL10: Then \\(r = 50\\), but \\(T\\) is unknown until the 50th failure occurs. This is called Censored Type II data. Censored Type II Data We observe \\(t_1, \\, t_2, \\, \\ldots, \\, t_r\\), where \\(r\\) is specified in advance. The test ends at time \\(T = t_r\\), and \\((n - r)\\) units have survived. Again we assume it is possible to observe the exact time of failure for failed units.\nL11: Type II censoring has the significant advantage that you know in advance how many failure times your test will yield - this helps enormously when planning adequate tests. However, an open-ended random test time is generally impractical from a management point of view and this type of testing is rarely seen.\nL12: Sometimes we don't even know the exact time of failure  | Readout or Interval Data Sometimes exact times of failure are not known; only an interval of time in which the failure occurred is recorded. This kind of data is called Readout or Interval data and the situation is shown in the figure below: 【4†Image: Diagram demonstrating interval data】. Multicensored Data In the most general case, every unit observed yields exactly one of the following three types of information:\nL13:   * a run-time if the unit did not fail while under observation\nL14:   * an exact failure time\nL15:   * an interval of time during which the unit failed.\nL16: The units may all have different run-times and/or readout intervals.\nL17: Many special methods have been developed to handle censored data  | How do we handle censored data? Many statistical methods can be used to fit models and estimate failure rates, even with censored data. In later sections we will discuss the 【5†Kaplan-Meier】 approach, 【6†Probability Plotting】, 【7†Hazard Plotting】, 【8†Graphical Estimation】, and 【9†Maximum Likelihood Estimation】.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDORA | Capabilities: Working in small batches (https://dora.dev/capabilities/working-in-small-batches/)\nciteturn0view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://dora.dev/capabilities/working-in-small-batches/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 102\nL0:   * 【0†AI】\nL1:   * 【1†Research】\nL2:   * 【2†Capabilities】\nL3:   * 【3†Guides】\nL4:   * 【4†Insights】\nL5:   * 【5†Quick Check】\nL6:   * search\nL7:   * 【6†Community  open_in_new†dora.community】\nL8: \nL9: [Input: Search dora.dev for...][Input]\nL10: \nL11: [Input] menu\nL12: \nL13:   * 【0†AI】\nL14:   * 【1†Research】\nL15:   * 【2†Capabilities】\nL16:   * 【3†Guides】\nL17:   * 【4†Insights】\nL18:   * 【5†Quick Check】\nL19:   * 【7†Search】\nL20:   * 【6†Community  open_in_new†dora.community】\nL21: \nL22: 【2†Capabilities】\nL23: # Working in small batches 【8†core】 【9†AI】\nL24: Working in small batches is an essential principle in any discipline where feedback loops are important, or you want to learn quickly from your decisions. Working in small batches allows you to rapidly test hypotheses about whether a particular improvement is likely to have the effect you want, and if not, lets you course correct or revisit assumptions.\nL25: Although this article applies to any type of change that includes organizational transformation and process improvement, it focuses primarily on software delivery.\nL26: Working in small batches is part of lean product management. Together with capabilities like 【10†visibility of work in the value stream】, 【11†team experimentation】, and 【12†visibility into customer feedback】, working in small batches predicts software delivery performance and organizational performance.\nL27: > In the era of generative AI, this capability has become even more critical. DORA research shows that working in small batches amplifies the positive impact of AI adoption on product performance and helps turn AI’s neutral effect on organizational friction into a net positive.\nL28: One reason work is done in large batches is because of 【13†the large fixed cost of handing off changes】. In traditional phased approaches to software development, handoffs from development to test or from test to IT operations consist of whole releases: months worth of work by teams consisting of tens or hundreds of people. With this traditional approach, collecting feedback on a change can take weeks or months.\nL29: In contrast, modern practices, which utilize cross-functional teams and lightweight approaches, allow for software to progress from development through test and operations into production in a matter of minutes. However, this rapid progression requires working with code in small batches.\nL30: \nL31: Working in small batches has many benefits:\nL32:   * It reduces the time it takes to get feedback on changes, making it easier to triage and remediate problems.\nL33:   * It increases efficiency and motivation.\nL34:   * It prevents your organization from succumbing to the sunk-cost fallacy.\nL35:   * It acts as a safety net for AI adoption. AI adoption often leads to increased 【14†software delivery instability】; working in small batches acts as a critical countermeasure to this risk, ensuring that the increased velocity from AI translates into value rather than chaos.\nL36: You can apply the small batches approach at the feature and the product level. As an illustration, a minimum viable product, or MVP, is a prototype of a product with just enough features to enable validated learning about the product and its business model.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://blog.crisp.se/wp-content/uploads/2012/11/SpotifyScaling.pdf)\nciteturn0view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://blog.crisp.se/wp-content/uploads/2012/11/SpotifyScaling.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 206\nL0@P0: Scaling Agile @ Spotify\nL1@P0: with Tribes, Squads, Chapters & Guilds\nL2@P0: Henrik Kniberg & Anders Ivarsson\nL3@P0: Oct 2012\nL4@P0: Dealing with multiple teams in a product development organization is always a challenge!\nL5@P0: One of the most impressive examples we’ve seen so far is Spotify, which has kept an agile mindset despite\nL6@P0: having scaled to over 30 teams across 3 cities.\nL7@P0: Spotify is a fascinating company that is transforming the music industry. The company has only existed 6\nL8@P0: years and already has over 15 million active users and over 4 million paying. The product itself can be\nL9@P0: likened to “a magical music player in which you can instantly find and play every song in the world”.\nL10@P0: Alistair Cockburn (one of the founding fathers of agile software development) visited Spotify and said “Nice -­\nL11@P0: I've been looking for someone to implement this matrix format since 1992 :) so it is really welcome to see.”\nL12@P0: So how is this managed?\nL13@P0: We have both presented at conferences and been caught in engaging discussions around how we work at\nL14@P0: Spotify and how the company handles agile with hundreds of developers. Many people are fascinated by\nL15@P0: this, so we decided to write an article about it.\nL16@P0: Disclaimer: We didn’t invent this model. Spotify is (like any good agile company) evolving fast. This article\nL17@P0: is only a snapshot of our current way of working -­ a journey in progress, not a journey completed. By the\nL18@P0: time you read this, things have already changed.\nL19@P0-1: 1/14Squads\nL20@P1: The basic unit of development at Spotify is the Squad.\nL21@P1: A Squad is similar to a Scrum team, and is designed to feel like a mini-­startup. They sit together, and they\nL22@P1: have all the skills and tools needed to design, develop, test, and release to production. They are a\nL23@P1: self-­organizing team and decide their own way of working – some use Scrum sprints, some use Kanban,\nL24@P1: some use a mix of these approaches.\nL25@P1: Each squad has a long-­term mission such as building and improving the Android client, creating the Spotify\nL26@P1: radio experience, scaling the backend systems, or providing payment solutions. The picture below\nL27@P1: illustrates how different squads take responsibility for different parts of the user experience.\nL28@P1: Squads are encouraged to apply Lean Startup principles such as MVP (minimum viable product) and\nL29@P1: validated learning. MVP means releasing early and often, and validated learning means using metrics and\nL30@P1: A/B testing to find out what really works and what doesn’t. This is summarized in the slogan “Think it, build\nL31@P1: it, ship it, tweak it”.\nL32@P1-2: 2/14Because each squad sticks with one mission and one part of the product for a long time, they can really\nL33@P2: become experts in that area -­ for example what it means to build an awesome radio experience.\nL34@P2: Most squads have an awesome workspace including a desk area, a lounge area, and a personal \"huddle\"\nL35@P2: room. Almost all walls are whiteboards. We've never seen a better collaboration space!\nL36@P2: yes, that's a shark flying around. perfectly normal.\nL37@P2: To promote learning and innovation, each squad is encouraged to spend roughly 10% of their time on “hack\nL38@P2: days”. During hack days people do whatever they want, typically trying out new ideas and sharing with their\nL39@P2: buddies. Some teams do 1 hack day every second week, others save up for a whole “hack week”. Hack\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMigrating to Cloud-Native Application Architectures (https://www.f5.com/content/dam/f5/corp/global/pdf/ebooks/Migrating_to_Cloud-Native_Application_Architecutres_NGINX.pdf)\nciteturn0view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 58; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.f5.com/content/dam/f5/corp/global/pdf/ebooks/Migrating_to_Cloud-Native_Application_Architecutres_NGINX.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1626\nL0@P1: Built and optimized to run on\nL1@P1: cloud and containers\nL2@P1: Advanced load balancing\nL3@P1: and caching\nL4@P1: Use independently or with\nL5@P1: load balancing services\nL6@P1: Custom health checks for\nL7@P1: high availability\nL8@P1: Visibility into the performance\nL9@P1: of your infrastructure\nL10@P1: Let us be your guide.\nL11@P1: Migrating to\nL12@P1: the cloud?\nL13@P1: Learn more at:\nL14@P1-2: nginx.com/cloudMatt Stine\nL15@P2: Migrating to Cloud-Native\nL16@P2-3: Application Architectures978-1-491-95483-6\nL17@P3: [LSI]\nL18@P3: Migrating to Cloud-Native Application Architectures\nL19@P3: by Matt Stine\nL20@P3: Copyright © 2015 O’Reilly Media. All rights reserved.\nL21@P3: Printed in the United States of America.\nL22@P3: Published by O’Reilly Media, Inc., 1005 Gravenstein